{
  "image_name": "53d2265ea06340e808231973ed55c116.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes two contrasting ways viruses influence carbon and nutrient flow around marine phytoplankton in the surface ocean. On the left, the “viral shunt” shows viruses infecting phytoplankton, producing infected cells that eventually lyse and release dissolved organic matter (DOM), nutrients, and many progeny viruses. That material is then taken up by heterotrophic bacteria, linking viral infection to bacterial respiration and recycling of carbon as CO₂ rather than export to depth. The arrows emphasize rapid local recycling among phytoplankton, bacteria, viruses, and dissolved material.\n\nOn the right, the “viral shuttle” highlights a different outcome in which infection appears to promote aggregation of cells and production of sticky material such as TEP, cell debris, membrane vesicles, and test fragments. These combine into large sinking particles that carry carbon downward into the deep ocean. Some infected or uninfected cells are also consumed by grazers, transferring material to higher trophic levels, while some CO₂ is still produced through respiration. Overall, the image contrasts viral processes that either retain nutrients and carbon in the microbial loop near the surface or enhance sinking export to the mesopelagic and deep ocean.",
